  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The weight, in kilograms, of sugar in a randomly selected bag with a capacity of $1\\,\\text{kg}$\n- The number of students in a randomly selected classroom\n- The outcome of flipping a coin",
  "worked_solution": "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nSome common examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,1].$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\u2026,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are heads or tails, which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
